<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ikshvaku dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">225 CE – 340 CE (approx.) · capital Vijayapuri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Successors of the Satavahanas in the Krishna valley, whose queens funded the great Buddhist monasteries of Nagarjunakonda.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-ikshvakus">Ikshvakus</a>, claiming descent from the legendary solar dynasty, rose in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty</a> collapsed in the early 3rd century. Their founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasishthiputra-chamtamula">Vasishthiputra Chamtamula</a>, built the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a> in the valley now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings performed Vedic sacrifices, including the horse sacrifice, while the women of the royal house and their relatives were lavish patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Inscriptions record their gifts to stupas and monasteries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda">Nagarjunakonda</a>, which hosted monks from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and elsewhere. The site's limestone carvings belong to the Amaravati school. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virapurushadatta">Virapurushadatta</a> married into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>' family.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> rule ended in the early 4th century, probably through the expansion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abhira-dynasty">Abhira dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a> was flooded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna-sagar-dam">Nagarjuna Sagar</a> dam in the 1960s, after excavated monuments were moved to higher ground.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 225 CE – 340 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:64.18%;width:2.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>225 CE – 340 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vijayapuri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasishthiputra Chamtamula, Virapurushadatta, Ehuvala Chamtamula</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ikshvakus of Vijayapuri, Andhra Ikshvakus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=282&amp;focus=ikshvaku-dynasty" aria-label="Ikshvaku dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6813 1759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ikshvaku dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7133 2015L7165 1820M7133 2015L7188 1956"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7165 1820h0M7188 1956h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7133 2015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7133 2015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vijayapuri. <a href="/atlas?year=282&amp;focus=ikshvaku-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
